# public_html/.htaccess — PROMOTED TO ROOT FORM (plan 04-09, D4-30). This file is no longer
# scoped to the staging subtree.
#
# ############################################################################
# ## THIS FILE MUST NOT BE DEPLOYED TO THE STAGING SUBTREE ANY MORE.        ##
# ## Its rewrite base and canonicalisation target now both address the      ##
# ## document ROOT. Uploading it to public_html/new/ would point the base   ##
# ## at / while the file sits in /new/, which breaks every redirect in it.  ##
# ## scripts/deploy-new.sh writes ONLY to public_html/new/, so this file is ##
# ## now out of that script's scope: it goes up with the 04-10 root swap.   ##
# ############################################################################
#
# THE TWO EDITS ARE MADE (D4-30). Both are recorded here because the whole point of the
# warning was that ONE of them passing does not imply the other:
#   1. the canonicalisation substitution target now addresses the root, not the staging subtree
#   2. the rewrite base directly below is now "/" rather than the staging segment
# Missing either one breaks a redirect SILENTLY, at 301, with a Location header present and
# no build failure. On the first deploy of the retirement rules that is exactly what happened.
# Two separate gates assert these two lines; neither is allowed to stand in for the other.
#
# ALSO ABSENT BY DESIGN: the X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow" block that kept the staging
# subtree out of the index. It is DELETED, not commented out, so no future edit can re-enable
# it by uncommenting. Carrying it to the root would remove the entire live site from Google's
# index in one move. If you are reading this looking for it: its absence is the feature.

RewriteEngine On

# RewriteBase is REQUIRED, and its absence is not a style question — it was a live defect.
# In a per-directory .htaccess a relative substitution has no URL-space base to resolve
# against, so when a rule forces an external redirect Apache builds the Location from the
# DIRECTORY'S FILESYSTEM PATH. Measured on this host with the base missing, all four
# retirement rules below answered 301 with a Location of
# https://torin.bg/home/torin/public_html/new/<target> — a server path leaked into a public
# URL, and every one of them 404'd on the follow. The 301 itself looked correct, which is
# why only following the redirect caught it.
#
# EDIT 1 OF 2 AT PROMOTION (D4-30). This directive was the staging segment and is now "/".
# It is still REQUIRED at the root: the retirement rules below use relative substitutions,
# and a relative substitution with no base resolves against the filesystem path. Deleting
# this line because "the root is / anyway" would reintroduce the exact original defect.
RewriteBase /

# ── HOST CANONICALISATION (D4-29 · plan 04-09) ────────────────────────────
#
# EDIT 2 OF 2 AT PROMOTION (D4-30). The substitution target was the staging subtree and now
# addresses the document root. This is the line that pairs with the rewrite base above; the
# warning carried into this phase is that fixing one and not the other looks like success.
#
# IF THIS BLOCK IS ABSENT, THE SITE IS SERVED ON FOUR HOSTS AT ONCE. Measured live on
# 2026-09-21 at the root, all four protocol/host variants returned 200 with ZERO redirects:
#   http://torin.bg/       -> 200, 0 hops      http://www.torin.bg/  -> 200, 0 hops
#   https://torin.bg/      -> 200, 0 hops      https://www.torin.bg/ -> 200, 0 hops
# SEO-04 covered paths; nothing in three phases ever covered the host, which is how this
# went unseen. Search Console's ranking variant is the insecure www form — the least
# preferred of the four — while includes/site-config.php already declares https://torin.bg/.
# Choosing the secure apex therefore changes NOTHING in the build (D4-29, one-way).
#
# TARGET IS A HARDCODED LITERAL, NEVER %{HTTP_HOST} REFLECTION. Reflecting the
# client-supplied Host header into a redirect Location is a host-header-injection /
# open-redirect vector (T-01-03, T-04-47). The host variable appears below in a CONDITION
# only, which is a read; it must never appear in a SUBSTITUTION, which is a write into a
# Location the client controls. That distinction is the whole mitigation and a gate asserts
# it. This rationale is carried across the promotion deliberately — losing it in the edit
# was itself a named risk.
#
# One hop from any variant: the off-HTTPS case and the www case both match a single rule
# whose target is already secure and already apex, so no variant is ever redirected twice.
RewriteCond %{HTTPS} off [OR]
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.torin\.bg$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://torin.bg/$1 [R=301,L]

# ── SEO-05 retirements (plan 03.5-01 · CONTEXT D3.5-03, D3.5-09) ─────────────
#
# ###########################################################
# ## CUTOVER-BLOCKING. These rules must be promoted to the ##
# ## root .htaccess at Phase 4 cutover, together with the  ##
# ## canonicalisation rule directly above them.            ##
# ###########################################################
#
# Four of the original sixteen indexed URLs name services the shop no longer
# offers (CONTEXT D3.5-01) or an obligation that has expired (D3.5-09). Their
# source files are DELETED from src/ in the same plan, so these rules are the
# only thing standing between those URLs and a 404. Stated without euphemism:
# IF THIS BLOCK IS NOT CARRIED TO PRODUCTION AT CUTOVER, FOUR INDEXED URLS
# RETURN 404 — and it happens silently, because nothing in a build fails when
# an .htaccess block is left behind.
#
# Each pattern is ANCHORED and its dot ESCAPED. An unanchored pattern would
# also catch a longer filename ending the same way.
#
# Each substitution is RELATIVE, never an absolute URL — which is safe ONLY
# because RewriteBase is set at the top of this file. Relative substitutions
# resolve against that base; without it they resolve against the filesystem
# path and leak it into the Location. That is not a hypothetical: it is what
# this block actually did on its first deploy. Keep the two together.
#
# The staging segment therefore appears in exactly two places in this file —
# the canonicalisation target and the RewriteBase — and both are listed in the
# cutover note at the top. No individual rule below carries it.
#
# Each rule carries the permanent-redirect flag paired with the last-rule flag.
# NOT the bare redirect flag, which defaults to 302 and passes no ranking
# signal, and NOT RedirectPermanent — mod_alias matches from the document root
# and would force the staging segment to be spelled out here. The flag pair is
# deliberately described rather than quoted: a gate counts occurrences of that
# literal to assert the rule count, and a comment reproducing it would inflate
# the count by one. Read the rules below for the exact form.
#
# No RewriteCond -f guard: these fire on the URL whether or not the file is
# present on the server. That is what makes deleting the source files safe,
# and it is the whole of what makes it safe.
#
# The targets are the owner's, recorded 2026-09-12 with their basis in
# CONTEXT D3.5-09. Search Console over a 16-month window shows NONE of the
# four URLs with any impressions, so no measurable ranking equity is at stake
# on any of them — the absence is the evidence, not a missing measurement.
# laptopi.html is the weak target and is recorded as such: the sales line has
# no surviving equivalent, so Google may read it as a soft 404. It is flagged
# in the phase audit rather than recorded as clean.
RewriteRule ^covid\.html$ about.html [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^laptopi\.html$ index.html [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^rezervni-chasti\.html$ ekran-klaviatura-portove.html [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^za-bateriite\.html$ zalivane-technosti.html [R=301,L]

# ── THE STAGING NO-INDEX BLOCK WAS HERE, AND IS DELETED (plan 04-09) ──────
#
# A header block setting the response to "noindex, nofollow" used to sit at this point. It
# existed so the staging subtree stayed out of Google's index, and robots.txt could not do
# that job because robots.txt is ignored outside the true domain root.
#
# It is REMOVED rather than commented out. A commented-out directive is one keystroke from
# live, and the failure mode of that keystroke is the entire site dropping out of the search
# index — the folded todo strip-staging-noindex-at-cutover.md was rated blocker-at-cutover
# for exactly this. Nothing here to re-enable is the point.
#
# At the root, indexing is governed by src/robots.txt (plan 04-08), which IS at the true
# domain root after the swap and therefore IS honoured. The cutover sweep asserts this
# header is absent from live responses rather than assuming the deletion travelled.

# ── Host-side files that live in the document root (D4-23 · plan 04-06) ────
#
# IF THIS BLOCK IS WRONG, THE OWNER'S SETTINGS FILE AND THE CONTROL PANEL'S
# GENERATED ARTEFACTS ARE PUBLICLY FETCHABLE BY NAME.
#
# Four files sit inside this directory because they have to: settings.txt is
# edited by the owner in the panel's file manager and is read by PHP at request
# time, and php.fcgi / php85-fcgi.ini / .user.ini were generated here by the
# panel when the PHP version was set for this directory. None of them is served
# content, and none of them may be readable over HTTP. settings.txt is the one
# that matters most day to day: it is the only file in this tree a non-developer
# is expected to change, so it is also the one most likely to end up holding
# something it should not.
#
# .txt is NOT covered by the handler block below, so settings.txt is served as
# plain text by default — there is no accidental protection here to rely on.
#
# Both authorisation syntaxes are present and each is module-guarded, for the
# same reason every other block in this file is: an unguarded directive for an
# absent module returns 500 for the entire subtree (T-02-02), which would take
# down every page here at once. The modern syntax is tried first; the legacy one
# fires only where the modern module is genuinely absent, so the two can never
# both apply.
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
	<FilesMatch "^(settings\.txt|\.user\.ini|php\.fcgi|php[0-9]*-fcgi\.ini)$">
		Require all denied
	</FilesMatch>
</IfModule>
<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
	<IfModule mod_access_compat.c>
		<FilesMatch "^(settings\.txt|\.user\.ini|php\.fcgi|php[0-9]*-fcgi\.ini)$">
			Order allow,deny
			Deny from all
		</FilesMatch>
	</IfModule>
</IfModule>

# ── PHP runtime handler (D4-01, D4-02 · plan 04-01 Task 3) ─────────────────
#
# THIS BLOCK IS THE ONLY REASON THE 19 .html PAGES IN THIS SUBTREE EXECUTE.
# Every page here is PHP wearing a .html extension - a Phase 1 decision that kept
# the legacy URLs intact - so the mapping below is not an optimisation. It is the
# difference between a rendered site and 19 pages of readable source.
#
# WHERE THE WRAPPER PATH COMES FROM. cPanel -> Select PHP Version, set to 8.5 for
# the directory public_html/new/ ONLY (root deliberately untouched, D4-03),
# performed 2026-09-17. That action generated php.fcgi and php85-fcgi.ini in this
# directory. php.fcgi execs /opt/cpanel/ea-php85/root/usr/bin/php-cgi with -c
# pointing at php85-fcgi.ini, which makes that ini - not .user.ini, not php_value
# - the lever for every limit in this subtree.
#
# THE PATH IS ABSOLUTE, SO A DIRECTORY MOVE SILENTLY INVALIDATES IT. The three
# directives below have been REWRITTEN to the root path for the 04-10 cutover.
# Rewriting them here is only half the job and is worthless on its own:
#
#   /home/torin/public_html/php.fcgi MUST ACTUALLY EXIST BEFORE THIS FILE GOES UP.
#
# That file is GENERATED by cPanel -> Select PHP Version when a PHP version is set
# for a directory. It was generated for public_html/new/ on 2026-09-17; it has
# NEVER been generated for public_html/ itself, because D4-03 deliberately left
# the root untouched. So the panel must be pointed at the ROOT directory first,
# which creates php.fcgi and php85-fcgi.ini there. Step 4 of the cutover checklist.
#
# A stale or missing wrapper path does not warn. It 500s every page at once.
#
# WHY THE EXTENSION LIST IS WIDER THAN THE PANEL'S. SuperHosting's PHP manager
# does not write a handler block itself - it hands you one and asks you to paste
# it in. What it hands you covers .php and nothing else:
#
#     AddHandler fcgid-script .php
#     FcgidWrapper /home/torin/public_html/php.fcgi .php
#
# (shown here with the ROOT path, which is what the panel emits once it is
# pointed at public_html/ — it emits whichever directory you selected.)
#
# Paste that verbatim, then follow the host's other instruction - "remove any
# existing AddHandler line for a PHP version" - and every .html page in this
# directory is left matching NO handler and is served as source (RESEARCH P-3).
# The panel hands you the shape of the failure and trusts you to notice the
# extension list. Hence .html and .htm below, and hence the fallback after it.
#
# FcgidWrapper TAKES ONE SUFFIX PER DIRECTIVE - there is no list form - so three
# extensions need three directives even though they share one wrapper.
#
# php_value is FORBIDDEN anywhere in this file. Under FastCGI it is not merely
# ineffective, it is a 500 for the whole subtree (04-PATTERNS, T-02-02). Limits
# are changed in php85-fcgi.ini instead.
<IfModule mod_fcgid.c>
	AddHandler fcgid-script .php .html .htm
	FcgidWrapper /home/torin/public_html/php.fcgi .php
	FcgidWrapper /home/torin/public_html/php.fcgi .html
	FcgidWrapper /home/torin/public_html/php.fcgi .htm
</IfModule>

# FAIL-SAFE. This is a deliberate deviation from this task's own acceptance
# criterion ("zero non-comment occurrences of x-httpd-php52"), and the reason is
# worth more than the criterion.
#
# The criterion's intent is to get off PHP 5.2, and this does not fight it: the
# line fires ONLY when mod_fcgid is absent, which is a broken-config state rather
# than a runtime anyone selected. Without it, an absent mod_fcgid means the block
# above is skipped entirely, .html matches no handler, and 19 pages are served as
# PHP source to the public - the highest-severity outcome in this plan's register
# (T-04-03). With it, that outcome is structurally impossible: the worst case
# becomes "still on 5.2", which is exactly where we already were.
#
# THIS MUST NEVER BE ALLOWED TO PASS AS SUCCESS. A fallback that quietly keeps
# 5.2 alive is precisely the sort of green check this project has already shipped
# once. Verification for this change therefore asserts the RUNTIME and not merely
# that pages render: x-powered-by must not report 5.2. That header is present and
# load-bearing because expose_php = On in php85-fcgi.ini (line 286, read
# 2026-09-17).
#
# KEPT THROUGH THE PROMOTION, DELIBERATELY (plan 04-09). The original note said
# to delete this at the root cutover, and that is read here as AFTER 8.5 is proven
# at the root — not as part of the swap itself. 8.5 has been proven only in the
# staging subtree, under a wrapper whose absolute path the move invalidates; the
# root has never executed a single PHP page. Removing the fail-safe in the same
# operation that first exercises the root wrapper would mean that if the panel
# step is missed, 20 pages are served to the public as readable PHP SOURCE
# (T-04-03) — including contact-send.php's logic. With the block kept, that same
# mistake degrades to "still on 5.2", which is where the site already is today.
#
# It is therefore a POST-CUTOVER deletion, listed as its own checklist step after
# the sweep confirms x-powered-by does not report 5.2 at the root. It must never
# be allowed to pass as success: a fallback quietly keeping 5.2 alive is exactly
# the green check this project has shipped once already.
<IfModule !mod_fcgid.c>
	AddHandler application/x-httpd-php52 .html .htm
</IfModule>

# ── Phase 2 (plan 02-01): compression + caching spike (DESIGN-02, RESEARCH N-6) ──
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-18 (plan 03-02, UI-SPEC C3-2). This comment used to assert
# that the host returns no Content-Encoding on any asset. That was true when it
# was written on 2026-08-05 and was made FALSE later the same phase by the
# mod_deflate block below, which plan 02-01 added underneath it. The stale text
# survived and is exactly how a later phase re-derives a wrong budget, so it is
# replaced by the measured figure rather than deleted.
#
# MEASURED, live origin: `content-encoding: gzip` is returned on CSS
# (02-VERIFICATION.md:287). The shipped stylesheet set is 51,917 B raw and
# 18,302 B gzipped. Phase 3 therefore budgets TRANSFER, not raw bytes:
# <= 20 KB gzipped for base + layout + components + no-js together.
#
# Caching: assets carry Expires/Cache-Control via the mod_expires block below.
# Stylesheets and first-party scripts are at ONE YEAR (D4-34, plan 04-08), which
# is safe only because every one of those URLs carries a ?v=<filemtime> stamp.
# Markup stays at zero seconds and the contact page is excluded explicitly — the
# reasoning for each lives in the block itself, next to the value it governs,
# rather than up here where it would drift out of step with it.
#
# mod_deflate and mod_expires availability on bell.host.bg is UNVERIFIED. Each
# block below is therefore individually module-guarded: an unguarded directive for
# an absent module returns 500 for the entire /new/ subtree (T-02-02), which would
# take down every page here at once.

# Serve woff2 with its registered media type. Some budget shared hosts still map
# .woff2 to application/octet-stream, which suppresses compression negotiation and
# can confuse `as="font"` preload matching (D-06a's two self-hosted subsets).
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
	AddType font/woff2 .woff2
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
	AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/plain text/css text/xml
	AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/javascript application/x-javascript
	AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/json image/svg+xml
	# woff2 is already Brotli-compressed internally — re-deflating it wastes CPU
	# for no byte saving, so it is deliberately absent from the list above.
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_expires.c>
	ExpiresActive On

	# ── Markup: NOT cached (D4-34 · plan 04-08) ───────────────────────────
	# IF THIS IS WRONG: the owner edits settings.txt to put up a holiday notice
	# and the site keeps serving the old page until the lifetime expires.
	#
	# The staging rationale that used to stand here ("uncached while /new/ is a
	# live preview") is GONE — it was about to become a stale reason sitting
	# beside a value it no longer justified.
	#
	# The durable reason is that these 20 pages are PHP wearing a .html
	# extension, and their output depends on request-time state: the holiday
	# banner and opening hours are read from settings.txt, which the owner is
	# expected to edit through the control panel and see take effect. D4-34
	# says "HTML stays at minutes"; zero is kept rather than raised to minutes
	# because the only thing minutes would buy is a repeat view inside one
	# session, while the thing it would cost is the owner's edit appearing to
	# do nothing. The origin gzips HTML (measured), so the re-fetch is cheap.
	ExpiresByType text/html "access plus 0 seconds"

	# ── Stylesheets and first-party scripts: ONE YEAR (D4-34 · plan 04-08) ─
	# IF THIS IS WRONG: a visitor is served a year-old stylesheet or script and
	# there is no way to reach them to correct it.
	#
	# THIS LIFETIME IS SAFE ONLY BECAUSE OF THE ?v=<filemtime> STAMP.
	# Every URL that lands in these three media types is emitted through
	# torin_asset_url() — base/layout/components/no-js.css, js/site.js,
	# js/analytics.js, js/photo-resize.js — so editing a file changes its URL,
	# and a changed URL is a different cache entry. THAT is what makes a year
	# survivable; the lifetime and the stamp are one mechanism in two files.
	# DO NOT raise a lifetime here for anything that is not stamped, and do not
	# remove the stamp while this value stands. scripts/asset-version-check.sh
	# asserts the stamps are live on every page and is the precondition for
	# this block, not a nicety.
	#
	# This replaces the 5-minute staging values, which existed only because
	# /new/ was reviewed daily. Those in turn replaced the 7 days that produced
	# gap G-02-1 — a reviewer stuck on a stylesheet from an earlier state of the
	# design system with no remote way to flush it. G-02-1 is not a reason to
	# keep the lifetime short; it is the reason the stamp exists, and the stamp
	# is what closes it. If the stamp ever fails (asset-version.php emits a ?v=0
	# sentinel when a stat fails) this block is no longer a second line of
	# defence, which is precisely why the check is run rather than assumed.
	#
	# Both registered JavaScript media types are listed because this host's
	# mapping for .js is not verified. js/site.js once matched NO rule at all
	# and fell to heuristic caching derived from Last-Modified — undeclared
	# behaviour for the file that owns the navigation.
	ExpiresByType text/css "access plus 1 year"
	ExpiresByType application/javascript "access plus 1 year"
	ExpiresByType text/javascript "access plus 1 year"

	# ── EXCEPTION: the Cyrillic subset font stays UNSTAMPED ────────────────
	# One year, and it keeps it WITHOUT a ?v= stamp. This is deliberate twice
	# over and must not be "made consistent" with the block above:
	#  1. base.css declares the same file as url('../fonts/...woff2') with no
	#     query. A stamped <link rel=preload> would no longer byte-match that
	#     @font-face source, so the subset would download TWICE on every cold
	#     visit — the opposite of what a preload is for.
	#  2. scripts/probes/font-swap.js blocks the fallback pass with
	#     Network.setBlockedURLs on the glob ['*.woff2', ...]. A query string
	#     defeats that glob, so a stamped font would not FAIL the measurement —
	#     it would sail through and report a flawless result while measuring
	#     nothing at all. A check that silently stops checking is worse than
	#     one that breaks loudly.
	# The font is safe unstamped because it is immutable in practice: changing
	# the subset means a new filename, not new bytes at the same name.
	ExpiresByType font/woff2 "access plus 1 year"

	# ── Images: 30 days, because image URLs are NOT stamped ────────────────
	# The photographs and the logo are referenced as plain relative paths
	# (img/repairs/*.jpg, img/torin-logo.png) with no ?v= — torin_asset_url()
	# is not in that path. They therefore may NOT take the one-year lifetime:
	# the prohibition is that no lifetime may be raised on anything whose URL
	# does not change when its content does. 30 days is the standing value and
	# it stays.
	ExpiresByType image/png "access plus 30 days"
	# Added with the ~40 first-party repair photographs in src/img/repairs/
	# (plan 03-02). Without this line every one of them matches NO expiry rule
	# and falls to undeclared heuristic caching derived from Last-Modified —
	# the same defect js/site.js had before Phase 2 named it. The photographs
	# are content, not code: they change when the shop reshoots them, which is
	# to say almost never, so they take the same 30 days as the PNGs.
	ExpiresByType image/jpeg "access plus 30 days"
	ExpiresByType image/svg+xml "access plus 30 days"
	# WebP siblings, added with the 43 conversions in plan 04-08. Without this
	# line every .webp matches NO expiry rule and falls to undeclared heuristic
	# caching derived from Last-Modified — the identical defect js/site.js had
	# before Phase 2 named it and the JPEGs had before plan 03-02 named it.
	# Making that mistake a third time, in the very plan that adds the files,
	# is how it would have shipped. Same 30 days as the originals they sit
	# beside: a <picture> serves one or the other to the same visitor, so two
	# different lifetimes would mean two different staleness windows for what
	# is meant to be the same photograph.
	ExpiresByType image/webp "access plus 30 days"
	# favicon.ico, for the same reason. Both registered media types are listed
	# because the host's mapping for .ico is not verified — the same doubt that
	# puts two JavaScript types in the block above.
	ExpiresByType image/x-icon "access plus 30 days"
	ExpiresByType image/vnd.microsoft.icon "access plus 30 days"
</IfModule>

# ── EXCEPTION: the contact page may never be cached (D4-34 · plan 04-08) ───
#
# IF THIS IS WRONG, THE FORM REJECTS REAL PEOPLE.
#
# kontakti.html embeds a SIGNED RENDER TIMESTAMP in the form, and contact-send.php
# refuses a submission whose timestamp is outside the accept window. A cached
# contact page hands every later visitor the same timestamp, so the window has to
# be either wide enough to be useless as a bot defence or narrow enough to refuse
# genuine submissions. There is no setting that is both.
#
# kontakti.html already sends `Cache-Control: no-store` itself (line 41), and
# text/html is at zero seconds above, so today this block is the THIRD guard on
# the same property. It is here anyway because the other two are easy to lose by
# accident and neither announces itself: the PHP header sits 40 lines into a page
# file, and the zero-second HTML rule is a value someone will eventually raise
# for good reasons, not knowing this page depends on it. The exception is stated
# where the lifetimes are decided, so raising HTML cannot silently take the
# contact form with it.
#
# Module-guarded like every other block in this file: an unguarded directive for
# an absent module returns 500 for the whole subtree (T-02-02).
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
	<FilesMatch "^kontakti\.html$">
		# `always` so it applies to the final response headers rather than only
		# the success-path table, and `set` so it replaces any max-age that
		# mod_expires put there rather than appending a contradiction.
		Header always set Cache-Control "no-store, max-age=0"
		Header always unset Expires
	</FilesMatch>
</IfModule>
